  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Lead Lost in One Inning",
    "what_this_means": "The Yankees held a lead through six innings and gave it back in the eighth. Schlittler was sharp. Five and a third innings, one run, seven strikeouts. But the bullpen folded. Tim Hill coughed up two runs before he could record an out. The offense managed four hits and nine strikeouts. They never made contact when it mattered. One run. That's what they had to show for a game they were winning.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what slipping looks like. The starter did his job. The pitching was good enough. The bullpen was not. But the bats were the real problem. Two runners left on base, no production in runners-in-scoring-position situations, and a lineup that went quiet after the second inning. They had their chances and they didn't convert them. This isn't a pattern yet. But this is how patterns start. Left runners on in the sixth, left them on again, and by the time the eighth inning came, there was nothing left. Four hits and nine strikeouts is not an offense. It's a warning.",
